WEBVTT

1
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.640
That's not a happy face.

2
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.680
- Well. it isn't a happy face.
- What happened?

3
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.200
- They're not going to do it at all?
- No.

4
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.680
Total refusal.

5
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.560
And the lawyer told the council...
All right. Alan.

6
00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.678
That's made us redundant then.
for the next six months.

7
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:43.620
You're redundant.

8
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:45.760
Fucking planners.

9
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.680
You must have
come across them a lot.

10
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:48.960
Fucking ridiculous.

11
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.360
Fifty plus people.
that would have employed.

12
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:52.420
Unbelievable.

13
00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.160
What about all the farmers
that would have employed. you know.

14
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:56.598
pork and stuff like that. local people?

15
00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:57.960
I know. We told them that.

16
00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.320
Crop to farm.
It's not gonna happen now. is it?

17
00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.520
And now we've got these cows

18
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.880
and absolutely no way
of selling them profitably.

19
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.720
We're committed now. aren't we?

20
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:08.820
- Investment.
- Yeah.

21
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:09.878
- 'Cause there was gonna be a restaurant.
- Yeah.

22
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.598
I know people at home
are going to be thinking

23
00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.400
"Why did he spend all this money
before he got planning permission?"

24
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.920
Well. if the restaurant
opened in April. May.

25
00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:18.280
whenever Alan got it built.

26
00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.158
you had to be able to sell something.
That's why we got the cows now.

27
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.798
'Cause I couldn't see a reason why
they'd turn the planning permission down.

28
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.840
I didn't know that that man
watches Sky At Night.

29
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.440
I couldn't see that that was
a relevant fact.

30
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.800
- You can appeal.
- Secretary of State.

31
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.718
What's a Secretary of State?
What does that mean?

32
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.478
Secretary of State
is the actual government.

33
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:36.420
Yeah.

34
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:37.560
We can't not do anything.

35
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.160
The next day at the farm shop.

36
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.400
Lisa and I were discussing the fallout.

37
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:50.120
So you know I worked out last night
how much I spent on the cows so far?

38
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.180
No.

39
00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.920
- Right. On the whole cow enterprise...
- Yeah.

40
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.760
Bearing in mind we were going to get

41
00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.720
£10.000 a cow in the restaurant.

42
00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.638
This is the cost so far
of the cow enterprise...

43
00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.680
£240.822.20!

44
00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:03.740
Yeah.

45
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.320
Nigh on a quarter of a million pounds.

46
00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.480
- Now. if you sell ten grand a cow...
- Yeah?

47
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.040
- Twenty-five cows...
- Yeah.

48
00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.840
Ten grand.
and we've paid for the cow enterprise.

49
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.440
If you were to sell a cow
to a supermarket.

50
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.520
you get twelve hundred quid. basically.
for a cow.

51
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.360
So. now I've got to sell 250 cows.

52
00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.240
And I haven't got 250 cows.

53
00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.040
I mean. it's gonna be thirty years...
thirty years before we turn a profit.

54
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.400
The other problem was that
our request to make a parking area

55
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.880
had been tied in
to the restaurant application.

56
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.800
So now. because it had all been refused.

57
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.240
we wouldn't be able to provide parking
for visitors to the farm shop.

58
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:48.920
The council's saying that this is an Area
of Outstanding Natural Beauty.

59
00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.800
which it isn't. because... look.

60
00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.000
And we say to them. "Can we have
permission to put some hardstanding down

61
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.800
"so that visitors can not chew
the fields up?"

62
00:02:58.560 --> 00:02:59.880
"No. you can't."

63
00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.600
And people can't go in that.
so they go and park on the verge.

64
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.520
If you come and look at this...

65
00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:13.040
So the locals complain that people are
parking on the verge and chewing them up.

66
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.000
Which is true. they are.

67
00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.960
Right. so we say to the council.
"Can we build a car park?"

68
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.120
"No. you can't."

69
00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:21.940
Back at the office.

70
00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.400
I discovered that even people
on their side of the fence

71
00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.080
agreed with me.

72
00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:31.520
I've had messages on social media
from councillors

73
00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.920
in other councils saying...

74
00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.040
that decision was idiotic

75
00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.040
and that I should take them to court.

76
00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.200
And I've had one
from Roger Daltrey saying.

77
00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.440
"Welcome to the land of no.

78
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.518
"I'm sorry to hear your planning
was turned down. Jeremy.

79
00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.518
"How dare you diversify."

80
00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.320
This is the lead singer of The Who.

81
00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.800
I'll listen more to him
than some... Herbert councillor.

82
00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.800
"I am putting in planning permission
for a microbrewery on my farm.

83
00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.680
"Water from a spring. hops and grain
from the local farms.

84
00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.758
"The cost of just putting in the planning
with all the environmental surveys.

85
00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.760
"newts. bats. earwigs and such:
£70.000.

86
00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.640
"How can the average farmer afford that?

87
00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:12.440
"Oh boy. can you have some fun with this
when you go to appeal.

88
00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.600
"Be lucky. mate. Roger."

89
00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.720
I then had to break the news
to our little cooperative.

90
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.360
- They've turned us down.
- Yeah. we heard.

91
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.000
That's really disappointing for us.

92
00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.080
We're desperate. on our knees.
to get rid of more pigs.

93
00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.080
Our farm's overflowing with pigs
at the moment.

94
00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.200
Pig farmers are suffering... Well.
I was about to say "more than anyone."

95
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.040
But Emma's got TB. so.

96
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.600
So how many...
You've lost sixty cows now?

97
00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.760
Yeah. Yeah. sixty in total.

98
00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.320
And you can't get rid of pigs.

99
00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:46.680
We can't get rid of them.

100
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.000
And the price is horrendous.
And the feed costs are astronomical.

101
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.560
We're cereal farmers
and we're buying in wheat

102
00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:55.720
at these astronomic prices
to feed the pigs.

103
00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:57.480
They're making no money.

104
00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:58.920
Jesus!

105
00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:02.360
I then went back to the farm.

106
00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:06.480
and got some bad news
about the thumb-flavoured crisps

107
00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.440
I'd been making from our potatoes.

108
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.560
"Environmental and regulatory services
of West Oxfordshire District Council.

109
00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.478
"According to the results.
the sample measures

110
00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:19.240
"10.500 micrograms of acrylamide."

111
00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:20.340
What?

112
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.120
My crisps are carcinogenic.

113
00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.360
How many people knew

114
00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:31.000
that a certain type of potato

115
00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:33.600
when cooked in a certain type of oil

116
00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:36.200
could perhaps cause cancer?

117
00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.200
How would you know that?

118
00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.720
Prepared from twenty...

119
00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:52.360
So if I want to make crisps.
I have to use a different type of potato.

120
00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:57.080
That means I've got...
How many tons of Melody potatoes

121
00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.400
have I got in cold storage?

122
00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.080
Which have started to sprout.

123
00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:04.600
Oh God!

124
00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:08.360
The only good news

125
00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.840
is that we couldn't actually sell
the crisps anyway

126
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.640
because at this point.
we didn't even have a farm shop.

127
00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:16.960
Right.
So we'll clear all this out.

128
00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:18.020
Yeah.

129
00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:22.200
The council had decided
that its traditional green-tin roof

130
00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:26.280
was an eyesore
and that it must be replaced with slate.

131
00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.560
So the shop had had to close
while the work was done.

132
00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:35.080
The problem we've got is because
we're having to put heavy slate on.

133
00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:39.320
these simple beams and rafters.
or whatever they're called.

134
00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:40.380
aren't strong enough.

135
00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.920
So we've gotta replace
the whole structure of the roof.

136
00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.118
It's almost like the council knew.

137
00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.800
"Can we put composite slate on."
which is light? "No.

138
00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.040
"Gotta be proper slate."

139
00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:52.320
It's. um...

140
00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:05.640
While the roof work
was going on.

141
00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:09.840
Charlie was busy preparing
a new separate planning application

142
00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:11.400
for a parking area.

143
00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:16.960
That car park is. you know.
absolutely vital

144
00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:19.480
to service the needs of the farm shop.

145
00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:23.240
And if we've ticked the box
with the roof.

146
00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:25.118
I'm really hopeful they turn around
and say.

147
00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:27.120
"Yeah. great. have your car park."

148
00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:32.640
That left me free to get on
with some actual farming.

149
00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:35.640
The hens didn't need much attention

150
00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:39.720
as they were still toughing it out
in bird-flu quarantine.

151
00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:42.560
To those waiting with baited breath

152
00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:46.760
for their favourite media catchphrase
"the U-turn".

153
00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:49.360
I have only one thing to say.

154
00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:51.920
You turn if you want to.

155
00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:58.520
But the new calves all needed
to have their ears pierced.

156
00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.040
And. as I didn't know how to do that.

157
00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:03.200
I had to ask Kaleb for help.

158
00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:06.320
These are the taggers. yeah?

159
00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:07.880
So that goes through their ear?

160
00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:11.800
Yeah. We've gotta find 10002.

161
00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:13.760
- What. in there?
- There it is. Look.

162
00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:15.360
Open up. open up. Relax.

163
00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:17.560
Give it a bit of a spray with iodine.

164
00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.000
Just to disinfect it.
And then go put it in his ear.

165
00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:22.600
- I'm not doing that.
- Why?

166
00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:26.640
Could you just... It's the first one.
I've gotta see it being done.

167
00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:28.960
Hold that one. then.
You do the tags. I'll do the ear.

168
00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:33.480
They're all a bit surprised
by this.

169
00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:35.520
Steady. steady. steady.

170
00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:37.678
I'm gonna get killed in a minute.
Let's do it quick.

171
00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:40.120
You get 'em like that. Yeah?

172
00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:46.238
It's all right. cow. Relax.
We should have brought some nuts...

173
00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:47.838
Right. number two.
We've got another one.

174
00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:49.200
- What?
- Quickly.

175
00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.160
What? Another one of what?

176
00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:53.158
The other number two.
We've gotta put two in their ears.

177
00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:56.360
You didn't tell me that.
There's thirty-seven. five...

178
00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:57.880
- I need two...
- Two!

179
00:08:58.520 --> 00:08:59.420
Got 'em?

180
00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.440
Please tell me
I've done this right.

181
00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:06.520
You've got new earrings!
Yes! Look at your earrings!

182
00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:09.400
It's like going to Accessorize. this is.

183
00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:10.720
Next one. quick.

184
00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:14.640
- No.
- Is that your testes?

185
00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:17.200
Yeah.

186
00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:19.360
I reckon you could definitely
tackle this calf.

187
00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:24.640
Right. come on. then. quickly.
Quickly. quickly.

188
00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:25.740
What?

189
00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:27.120
- You see them two marks?
- No!

190
00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:28.180
There. look.

191
00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:30.360
I haven't got my glasses on.
There?

192
00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:32.840
No. no. no.
Now. Squeeze hard.

193
00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.320
- Hard! Hard!
- She squeezed her ear up...

194
00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:38.840
- She squeezed her ear up...
- Hard. hard. That's it. Perfect.

195
00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:40.880
I did a thing!

196
00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:48.880
Once the tagging was complete.

197
00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.760
the numbers all had to be registered
with the government's cow police.

198
00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:55.040
So. Kaleb stayed in the shed

199
00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.320
and rang them through
to Charlie and me.

200
00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:00.640
- Right. Are you ready?
- Yeah.

201
00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:04.600
So. 300225...

202
00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:06.000
Yeah.

203
00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:08.080
Is the mother of

204
00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:11.640
50002.

205
00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.760
Whoa. whoa... 400002?

206
00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.000
- No. 5.
- 5.5?

207
00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:18.760
How can that be?

208
00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:23.360
Trust me. Hear me out.
hear me out. Chill out. Chill out.

209
00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:24.960
Right.

210
00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:26.200
And now. I don't know.

211
00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:33.840
The 400177...

212
00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:36.160
Yeah?

213
00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:43.880
225's heifer calf is which one?

214
00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:45.040
Number 1.

215
00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:46.880
No. that was 164's. Kaleb...

216
00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:47.940
Number 3.

217
00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:51.000
No. number 2.

218
00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:52.440
Oh my God. this is hard.

219
00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.040
The chances of these
being registered accurately: nought.

220
00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:01.080
I'm still not happy about 300225.

221
00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:02.620
That's the mother.

222
00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:04.920
Yeah? I just told you.
that's number 2.

223
00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:06.600
- No. you didn't.
- 3. sorry.

224
00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:09.400
Yeah...
Which one? Which one?

225
00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:11.640
Poppy's mum.
I just read her out. yeah?

226
00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:13.600
I don't know who Poppy is.

227
00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:15.640
- The first one. Number 1.
- Right.

228
00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:19.480
400177

229
00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:21.600
is the mother of

230
00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:26.760
40001.

231
00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:30.280
Last time you said 500164

232
00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:33.400
had calf 400001.

233
00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:34.560
2.

234
00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:36.840
That's number 2.

235
00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:40.800
No. seriously...

236
00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:43.920
I'm going from the beginning.

237
00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:45.640
Oh no!

238
00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:46.920
No! God!

239
00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:48.280
Has anybody got a gun?

240
00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:50.920
After all that.

241
00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:54.480
we assumed that registering
the numbers with the cow police

242
00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:56.720
would be a breeze.

243
00:11:57.080 --> 00:12:00.000
Thank you for calling
the British Cattle Movement Service.

244
00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:02.760
Many of our people
are still working from home.

245
00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:05.280
so you may hear additional
background noise.

246
00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:06.440
We've gone back to work.

247
00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:09.640
For information
on how we handle personal data.

248
00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:14.600
go to www.gov.uk
and search Rural Payments Agency...

249
00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:15.718
This is what we have to put up with.

250
00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:18.120
...Personal Information Charter.

251
00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:22.040
To report animal births.
movements and deaths to us.

252
00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:23.480
press one.

253
00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:25.680
Please tell me your county parish
holding number.

254
00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:27.200
That's the CPH.

255
00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:31.760
Three. three. slash. one. zero. two.

256
00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:35.320
slash. zero. one. six. zero.

257
00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:39.680
Thank you.
Was that: three. zero. one...

258
00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:41.480
No! No.

259
00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:43.440
...zero. two. zero. one?

260
00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:46.080
Okay. Sorry about that.
Let's try again.

261
00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:48.920
Please tell me your county parish
holding number.

262
00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:50.680
Three. three.

263
00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:53.240
one. zero. two.

264
00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:56.520
zero. one. six. zero.

265
00:12:57.560 --> 00:12:58.460
Thank you.

266
00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:02.520
Was that: three. three. one. zero. two.

267
00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:05.360
zero. one. six. zero?

268
00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:06.720
Yes.

269
00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:11.200
Right.
Please wait while I check those details.

270
00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:14.080
Sorry. there isn't a match
between the CPH number

271
00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:15.718
and the phone number you're calling from.

272
00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:16.660
No?

273
00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:20.080
I cannot
put you through to the BCMS helpline.

274
00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:22.040
For further help and information.

275
00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:24.320
please hang up
and call the following number

276
00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:26.400
during normal office hours.

277
00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:27.520
The number is:

278
00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:32.680
0345-050-1234.

279
00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:35.960
Charlie. that was all recorded. OK?

280
00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:38.080
I'm not doing it. I'm not doing it.

281
00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:40.680
They can piss off.

282
00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:43.320
I hate the government so much.

283
00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.200
Mercifully. I was scheduled
to spend the next day

284
00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:55.240
with a normal. nice. proper person

285
00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:57.040
who wasn't from the government.

286
00:13:57.680 --> 00:13:59.880
All right. looking for a man
called Robin.

287
00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:02.160
who is a hedge person.

288
00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:07.480
Robin is a scout for
the British Hedgelaying Society.

289
00:14:07.640 --> 00:14:10.480
And he'd come to see me
because Diddly Squat

290
00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:14.600
had been invited to host
a prestigious competition.

291
00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:17.040
There he is.

292
00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:19.080
Examining the hedge.

293
00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:21.520
Excellent.

294
00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:24.440
- Robin. Are you well?
- Always good.

295
00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:25.758
Have you found a suitable hedge?

296
00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:26.700
Yes.

297
00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:30.560
So we're going to lay this hedge.
with your permission.

298
00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:33.000
from here right way round to there.

299
00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:37.160
And we'll have thirty-two competitors
doing five different styles.

300
00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:39.200
You're basically making a fence
out of a hedge?

301
00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.480
Yes. Well. see. it's a craft.

302
00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:44.760
In my mind.
I was being rather cunning.

303
00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:48.000
because I assumed
I was getting a free fence.

304
00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:50.440
However...

305
00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:53.120
The competition.
we start it at nine.

306
00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:56.720
Five hours. You'll be very kind
to provide them with something to eat.

307
00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:58.800
- Oh. so I'm doing lunch?
- Yes. yes.

308
00:14:59.000 --> 00:14:59.900
How many people?

309
00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:00.940
Thirty-two.

310
00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:03.080
- Plus...
- And wives and partners.

311
00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:04.720
So it could be sixty? Seventy?

312
00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:06.880
- Yes. yes.
- So. big lunch.

313
00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:10.200
And does that tend to be in the house
or do we put up a marquee?

314
00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:13.960
- If you're gonna put up a marquee...
- A marquee.

315
00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:16.000
And at the end of this.

316
00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:19.480
you will put in the prize money.
etcetera.

317
00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:22.000
- So I do the prize money?
- Yes. you do.

318
00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.480
What sort of level of prize money
would it normally be?

319
00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:27.000
Per class. it's about £240.

320
00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:29.520
£240 per class and there's five classes?

321
00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:30.640
Five classes. yes.

322
00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:35.440
This news caused me
to break out my emergency first-aid kit.

323
00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:37.760
Oh my God!

324
00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:40.960
I'd been sitting
looking at the sloe gin...

325
00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:42.160
I mean the sloe berries.

326
00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:43.640
These are ones we made earlier.

327
00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:45.760
Oh my God. This is good.

328
00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:47.720
- Cheers!
- Cheers to you.

329
00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:53.080
That's good.

330
00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:55.120
- The sloe's out of there.
- Yeah.

331
00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:56.640
Mixed with a little bit of gin.

332
00:15:56.840 --> 00:16:00.360
There's a drink here... I don't know
if you've come across it. King's Ginger.

333
00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:03.480
Which I'm always amused by.

334
00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:06.080
It says here: "This ginger beverage

335
00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:09.600
"was specifically formulated
by Berry Brothers in 1903

336
00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:11.440
"for King Edward VII."

337
00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:14.760
"It was created to stimulate His Majesty

338
00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:17.680
"during morning rides
in his new horseless carriage."

339
00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:21.280
It was actually invented
for drinking and driving!

340
00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:24.800
- "This'll keep you warm. sir."
- Horseless carriage!

341
00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:27.360
In his new horseless carriage
in 1903.

342
00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:28.640
Yeah. yeah.

343
00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:31.200
So. my mine's evaporated.

344
00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:37.640
After Robin's hedge fund
had emptied my wallet

345
00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:39.400
and my drinks cabinet.

346
00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:42.920
I went off to see
the entrepreneurial foetus.

347
00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:45.280
who'd just invested in some new kit

348
00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:48.520
for his ever-growing farm
contracting business.

349
00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:50.960
What do you think?

350
00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:52.720
Have you got a new tractor?

351
00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:55.160
A bit small.

352
00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:56.920
What do you mean. "small"?

353
00:16:57.080 --> 00:16:59.240
210 horsepower.

354
00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:01.200
- Well. with boost.
- Yeah. Yeah.

355
00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:02.260
185.

356
00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:05.080
What do you think of the wheels. though?
I customised it.

357
00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:06.880
- Black wheels?
- Black wheels.

358
00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:09.360
I put a go-fast stripe on it
as well.

359
00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:11.280
Where is it?
Is it on the roof?

360
00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:13.680
No. on the bonnet.
So it starts here. look.

361
00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:16.680
So that's your go-faster stripe?

362
00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:18.720
- And it's on the top as well.
- Yeah. because...

363
00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:21.920
So the only evidence that you've got
a go-faster stripe is that.

364
00:17:22.080 --> 00:17:23.080
Yeah.

365
00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:26.640
185 horsepower.
How much was it?

366
00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:28.640
A hundred and four.

367
00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:33.200
Two and a half times more expensive
than my tractor

368
00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:35.760
and with a hundred horsepower less.

369
00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:37.520
Lifting capacity of this?

370
00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:38.960
Eight point seven-five?

371
00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:41.000
- Ten and a half.
- Yeah?

372
00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:42.560
I've got GPS.

373
00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:47.440
Right. And how long do you think
you're gonna have GPS before it's stolen?

374
00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:49.760
Oh. a couple of months.

375
00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:52.560
What's the longest
a farmer's ever owned GPS

376
00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:54.518
before they get up in the morning
and someone's nicked it?

377
00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:56.560
Probably six months
is the longest time.

378
00:17:57.560 --> 00:17:59.760
His piddly tractor's first job

379
00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:02.680
would be to help me plant
the spring barley.

380
00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:04.960
You're missing a few. um...

381
00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:06.160
pins. look.

382
00:18:06.680 --> 00:18:08.520
Which meant attaching the drill

383
00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:12.080
to my vastly superior Lambo.

384
00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:14.560
- Oh my God!
- What?

385
00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:16.760
It's a little shrew or something.

386
00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:19.440
- Oh. there he is! Oh wow!
- Get the gun.

387
00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:22.520
Don't be so unkind.

388
00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:25.838
- You're not frightened of mice?
- I don't like rats and mice.

389
00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:27.758
- It's not a rat. it's a mouse.
- Can you finish him off. please?

390
00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:28.918
I'm not gonna finish him off!

391
00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:30.760
And I'm not going out there
with him in there.

392
00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:33.080
Do you think that goes
all the way down?

393
00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:34.200
Yeah. yeah.

394
00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:35.440
Come on. little mouse.

395
00:18:36.040 --> 00:18:38.400
Just dangle it down there.
He'll be all right.

396
00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:42.080
This is kind to the environment
and kind to animals.

397
00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:45.480
Sir Attenborough would like me
for doing this.

398
00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:49.160
There he goes!
Mr Mouse!

399
00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:52.200
He's underneath your wheel.
He's actually underneath your wheel.

400
00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:54.080
As soon as you move.
you're gonna kill him.

401
00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:58.200
He's in the tyre tracks
of the tractor down there.

402
00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:00.240
Come on! Out.

403
00:19:00.440 --> 00:19:02.920
We can't afford to lose
any more time 'cause of the mouse.

404
00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:06.480
Oh bloody hell. I mean. there's a lot
of decisions in farming

405
00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:07.878
but this is a really nasty one.

406
00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:08.820
Just go.

407
00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:12.960
We've gotta go drilling.

408
00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:17.560
If I roll back a millimetre.

409
00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:20.960
Mr Mouse is toast.

410
00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:40.800
Now. that is how you do a hill start.

411
00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:43.240
But then.
when I lifted the drill...

412
00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:45.200
Oh shit. I've rolled back!

413
00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:52.640
You just killed that poor mouse.
I'll bury him.

414
00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:53.920
Back up.

415
00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:55.340
No. forwards.

416
00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:57.400
Is he stuck to the tyre?

417
00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:00.320
He lived!

418
00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:05.960
Meow!

419
00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:09.480
The mouse lives because I can drive.

420
00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:17.000
Crisis over.
we got down to planting.

421
00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:19.398
Look at this.
we are working as a team now.

422
00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:24.080
You're fertilising and I'm drilling
in the same field at the same time.

423
00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:27.800
And instead of expensive
chemical fertiliser...

424
00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:32.800
We were using my very own chicken muck.

425
00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:35.880
which made Kaleb very happy.

426
00:20:36.680 --> 00:20:40.320
My brand-new tractor's covered
in chicken shit. It stinks.

427
00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:46.960
The spring barley from here.
if the quality's good enough.

428
00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:51.040
will go to the brewery and will be made
into Hawkstone beer.

429
00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:53.880
That's why we have to do it
really. really. really

430
00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:55.800
to the best of our abilities.

431
00:20:57.480 --> 00:21:00.520
However. at the moment
there was neither a farm shop

432
00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:04.400
nor a restaurant
in which to sell that beer.

433
00:21:05.720 --> 00:21:08.160
But. over at the Royal Academy.

434
00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:12.240
Charlie was working on a plan
to sort that out.

435
00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:15.560
To take the restaurant appeal
to the next level

436
00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:18.440
is to take it to the Secretary of State.

437
00:21:18.560 --> 00:21:22.240
We've got one shot at this.
This is our last chance.

438
00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:27.400
So we need the best specialist advisors
to help with this.

439
00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:30.040
and. you know. barristers and lawyers.

440
00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:32.320
And I'm looking at. you know.
who they are now.

441
00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:36.560
By some miracle.

442
00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:41.160
Charlie had also managed to register
the calves with the cow police.

443
00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:45.720
And today was yet another milestone
in their adolescence.

444
00:21:45.920 --> 00:21:49.320
because two of them
were due to be dehorned.

445
00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:51.680
So I'm just gonna jab 'em.

446
00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:53.400
And this is what?
To calm them down?

447
00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:55.920
Just to make 'em...
They'll hopefully fall asleep.

448
00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:57.920
- Bring that gate round this way.
- What. now?

449
00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:00.040
- Yeah.
- That's the ticket.

450
00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:01.280
- This one first?
- Yeah.

451
00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:03.560
- Where does it go?
- In the neck.

452
00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:11.160
- Is this the other one?
- Yeah.

453
00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:15.040
Jolly good.

454
00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:18.200
We'll just leave 'em now for 10-15
minutes and they'll just fall asleep.

455
00:22:19.720 --> 00:22:21.840
Why do you get rid
of their horns?

456
00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:25.400
Because the ones with horns
become dominant and bully the rest.

457
00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:27.720
- And safety for us as well.
- Yeah.

458
00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:29.920
Oh. there you go. look.
He's down.

459
00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:31.160
Look at his face.

460
00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:33.120
Yeah. really sleepy.

461
00:22:33.320 --> 00:22:35.880
The mother looking all concerned. look.

462
00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:37.680
All right. mum. she's fine.

463
00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:40.640
There we go.

464
00:22:42.280 --> 00:22:44.160
- What's this? A local?
- Yes.

465
00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:46.518
Normally. when they're conscious
you're grappling with them now.

466
00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:50.720
But because we knocked 'em out.
it's a lot less stressful for the calf.

467
00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:56.480
Dilwyn's medical equipment
then became slightly less high-tech.

468
00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:05.800
Oh my giddy aunt.
You've set fire to her head.

469
00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:07.960
Holy cow!

470
00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:11.000
So you keep going round
in a circle.

471
00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:13.040
But there's actually flames.

472
00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:17.400
Aw. That must hurt.

473
00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:19.320
Aw!

474
00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:24.560
And this kills the horn?

475
00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:26.400
Yeah. it burns
the germinal layer.

476
00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:28.440
which is the bit of the horn that grows.

477
00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:30.800
So you get rid of the horn

478
00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:35.560
and also you get rid of the tissue
where the horn grows from.

479
00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:38.240
Is this iodine you're putting on there.
Kaleb?

480
00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:40.840
It's an antibiotic spray.
actually.

481
00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:42.520
to stop it becoming infected.

482
00:23:42.680 --> 00:23:45.920
And I'm gonna give her
an injection of a pain killer

483
00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:48.440
'cause one of the things
that sometimes happens

484
00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:52.560
is. as the anaesthetic wears off.
the head becomes tickly.

485
00:23:52.720 --> 00:23:55.280
I don't think anybody
who's ever had their head set on fire

486
00:23:55.440 --> 00:23:56.960
says. "It's a bit tickly."

487
00:23:57.640 --> 00:23:59.280
So there. that's one done.

488
00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:01.880
Poor little fella.

489
00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:05.120
Thankfully.
the coming weekend's activities

490
00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:08.160
promised to be much less stressful.

491
00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:24.360
Thirty-two of the country's
finest competitors.

492
00:24:24.520 --> 00:24:28.520
each allotted a nine-metre section
of unkempt hedge.

493
00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:31.320
would be making a living fence

494
00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:34.160
using five different styles.

495
00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:35.320
Dorset.

496
00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:36.800
North Somerset.

497
00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:37.920
Midland.

498
00:24:38.120 --> 00:24:39.760
Lancs &amp; Westmorland

499
00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:41.600
and Welsh.

500
00:24:41.800 --> 00:24:43.800
Welcome to Diddly Squat.

501
00:24:43.960 --> 00:24:46.000
Judging will take place through the day.

502
00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:48.800
The scores will be added up
on the computer this time.

503
00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:50.200
so there'll be no mistake.

504
00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:53.640
If you're all ready. go!

505
00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:05.120
These premier-league fencemakers
had five hours

506
00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:07.560
to complete their hedges.

507
00:25:12.760 --> 00:25:15.680
And it wasn't just a beauty contest.

508
00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:21.000
This is the traditional way
of creating a fence.

509
00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:23.160
an impenetrable fence. from a hedge.

510
00:25:23.320 --> 00:25:25.760
They've been doing it this way for...

511
00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:27.000
hundreds of years.

512
00:25:27.680 --> 00:25:30.520
The idea is. you cut the trunk.
but not completely.

513
00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:33.240
and then bend it over.

514
00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:34.560
Here we go. look.

515
00:25:36.920 --> 00:25:38.318
And he pulls them down.
Look at that.

516
00:25:38.360 --> 00:25:40.038
You just go. "Well. you've ruined that."
but he hasn't.

517
00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:42.640
They're still attached to the roots.

518
00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:44.520
But it'll now be going that way.

519
00:25:44.720 --> 00:25:47.640
And then you have to find another one
to sort of intertwine it with.

520
00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:51.440
And then you've got a living...

521
00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:53.920
Literally a living fence

522
00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:56.560
through which sheep can't pass.

523
00:25:58.240 --> 00:26:00.040
When they get the trunk down to that.
you say.

524
00:26:00.200 --> 00:26:01.838
"Well. don't bend that.
it's just going to snap."

525
00:26:01.880 --> 00:26:03.400
- But it doesn't.
- No.

526
00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:06.680
Eighty percent
is what you want to be looking for.

527
00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:09.040
- Eighty?
- Eighty percent cut.

528
00:26:09.240 --> 00:26:11.360
- You need twenty percent left?
- Yeah.

529
00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:12.598
And twenty percent is enough

530
00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.400
- to keep the plant alive?
- Yes.

531
00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:25.320
As the hedge-bound managers
toiled away.

532
00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:29.440
they were joined by a rival
from "Wall Street". and his wife.

533
00:26:30.680 --> 00:26:32.160
- Hi. Gerald.
- Good morning.

534
00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:34.280
- How are you. Mrs Gerald?
- Fine. thank you.

535
00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:35.880
- This is good. innit?
- Yeah. it is.

536
00:26:40.200 --> 00:26:41.100
Yeah.

537
00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:47.920
This. I don't know. I think I'll just...

538
00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:54.480
That's what I didn't like about the job.

539
00:26:56.360 --> 00:26:57.760
Well. I'm gonna have a try at this.

540
00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:02.280
In fact. Kaleb and I
both decided to have a try.

541
00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:03.880
Have you noticed again

542
00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:07.480
how we are hitting
every single diversity target

543
00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:09.200
that you have to hit on TV these days?

544
00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:13.040
Black. homosexual. transitioning.

545
00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:17.640
- That's what I used.
- Which do you want? That one?

546
00:27:17.800 --> 00:27:19.560
I mean. I've gotta have the feel for it.

547
00:27:19.720 --> 00:27:22.440
Why won't it cut...
It won't cut through this.

548
00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:28.360
Yeah. that's exactly what I was about
to do. Just show me again.

549
00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:30.720
Don't do it all for him.

550
00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:33.640
And again. Yeah!

551
00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:36.760
Having trimmed the branches.
on my own.

552
00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:38.840
I switched to the axe...

553
00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:39.900
which went well.

554
00:27:41.560 --> 00:27:43.080
Oh. God!

555
00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:44.840
It's thinner.
Shall I start at the top

556
00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:46.560
and then come down nicely like that?

557
00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:47.680
Yeah. That's nice.

558
00:27:47.840 --> 00:27:49.640
- A bit more?
- Yeah.

559
00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:51.520
Excellent. Excellent.

560
00:27:54.040 --> 00:27:55.440
I've got it.

561
00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:58.640
Jeremy?
Are you doing Midland style?

562
00:27:59.800 --> 00:28:02.520
I'm doing a Brazilian.
unless I get it wrong.

563
00:28:02.680 --> 00:28:04.720
in which case it's a Hollywood.

564
00:28:06.240 --> 00:28:07.718
You don't know what I'm talking about.
do you?

565
00:28:07.760 --> 00:28:10.040
Do you not know what a Brazilian is?

566
00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:12.200
I've never ever seen a Brazilian person.

567
00:28:13.080 --> 00:28:16.760
Mercifully. we were interrupted
at this point by Gerald.

568
00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:19.600
who wanted to inspect my handiwork.

569
00:28:19.760 --> 00:28:20.718
That looks great. doesn't it?

570
00:28:20.760 --> 00:28:22.560
Dear oh dear.

571
00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:24.800
But that. that... those...

572
00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:28.440
- What a load of shit.
- I mean...

573
00:28:28.600 --> 00:28:33.600
Every time you pull out you've gotta try
and get the baby between the stumps.

574
00:28:33.800 --> 00:28:36.000
You're never gonna...

575
00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:40.040
'Cause you're looking at cars and buses
and stuff all the time.

576
00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:43.040
You can't mow... You know.
it's like mowing grass.

577
00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:45.278
You go whoof. like that. one hit.
and it's all done. innit?

578
00:28:45.320 --> 00:28:46.440
Yeah.

579
00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:54.600
Having created nothing more
than a sodding great gap in the hedge.

580
00:28:54.800 --> 00:28:58.520
Kaleb and I went to see
how the experts were getting along.

581
00:29:07.680 --> 00:29:09.960
- Holy shit. look at this.
- That is amazing.

582
00:29:10.120 --> 00:29:13.480
This is absolutely stunning.
And this is Dorset?

583
00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:15.240
Birds can nest in this. can't they?

584
00:29:15.400 --> 00:29:17.600
Yes. It's good for wildlife.
And butterflies.

585
00:29:17.800 --> 00:29:21.000
Well. I think that's incredible.

586
00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:25.280
Oh. hello.
There's another beauty here. look.

587
00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:27.080
- And that's Midlands?
- Yes.

588
00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:29.278
Yeah. they made
the Austin Allegro and the Morris Marina.

589
00:29:29.320 --> 00:29:30.220
the Midlands.

590
00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:34.880
But they do give us the best hedge.
That's a beautiful hedge.

591
00:29:35.080 --> 00:29:37.160
Yeah. Is Midlands Birmingham?

592
00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:41.840
Eventually.
the five hours were up.

593
00:29:42.520 --> 00:29:46.360
The fencemakers went off
to have lunch in the marquee.

594
00:29:46.560 --> 00:29:50.960
And Simon Cowell and Amanda Holden
moved in to start judging.

595
00:29:51.160 --> 00:29:53.120
It's not following
the flow of the hedge.

596
00:29:53.320 --> 00:29:55.040
But otherwise.
they've done a very good job.

597
00:29:55.680 --> 00:29:57.480
I did not envy their task.

598
00:29:58.000 --> 00:30:00.480
because the whole length of the field

599
00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:02.880
was now a work of art.

600
00:30:24.400 --> 00:30:27.680
Judging over.
it was time for the prizegiving.

601
00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:31.640
Third place in the North Somerset class

602
00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:33.880
is Colin Clutterbuck.

603
00:30:34.280 --> 00:30:36.200
Well done. Colin.

604
00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:40.920
Second place in the North Somerset.
Alex Bown.

605
00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:43.640
With the runners-up sorted.

606
00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:46.680
Kaleb and I then had to announce
the overall winner.

607
00:30:46.840 --> 00:30:49.360
And we were very slick.

608
00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:52.800
Right. so. this is it.
The supreme champion.

609
00:30:53.600 --> 00:30:54.960
- Do we know who it is?
- Yes.

610
00:30:55.120 --> 00:30:56.480
I mean. we...

611
00:30:56.640 --> 00:30:59.480
The supreme champion today...

612
00:30:59.640 --> 00:31:01.720
I know. but I need to know the name.
'Cause we...

613
00:31:01.880 --> 00:31:02.960
Paul Gulliford.

614
00:31:05.520 --> 00:31:07.720
He's done it. Congratulations.

615
00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:09.640
Now you're gonna have
your local newspaper...

616
00:31:09.720 --> 00:31:10.998
I've gotta come round by you. have I?

617
00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:12.400
You hold that one end.

618
00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:16.798
Thank you all ever so much for coming.

619
00:31:16.840 --> 00:31:18.600
We hope you enjoyed your day

620
00:31:18.760 --> 00:31:21.680
and we hope you enjoy
watching yourselves on television.

621
00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:23.040
Well. you will 'cause you won.

622
00:31:23.240 --> 00:31:24.800
Everybody else is being edited out.

623
00:31:33.760 --> 00:31:37.440
On the Monday after
the wonderful hedge-laying weekend.

624
00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:38.920
I went to the farm shop.

625
00:31:39.080 --> 00:31:42.560
because Alan had finished the new roof.

626
00:31:49.160 --> 00:31:50.918
- Morning. Alan.
- Morning. Jeremy. All right?

627
00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:53.240
Good work.
You've done a bloody good job.

628
00:31:53.440 --> 00:31:55.160
That's the fastest
I've ever seen you move.

629
00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:56.720
- No. it's not.
- Yeah. it is.

630
00:31:56.880 --> 00:31:58.440
Yeah. that's bloody quick.

631
00:31:58.960 --> 00:32:01.680
Alan had also been busy
making the shop ready

632
00:32:01.840 --> 00:32:04.800
for the arrival of the fire inspector.

633
00:32:06.080 --> 00:32:08.600
Fire alarm's up there.
just if you don't know there's a fire.

634
00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:10.920
And a smoke alarm.
"Ah!" Panic. panic.

635
00:32:11.080 --> 00:32:13.200
- Is that the rules?
- Yeah. yeah. Listen.

636
00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:15.600
Fire extinguisher's there.
in case you wanna put it out.

637
00:32:15.680 --> 00:32:17.160
We had to put a light up there. look.

638
00:32:17.360 --> 00:32:19.800
Emergency light in case it gets dark
and we shut at four.

639
00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:24.040
And I put signs on the door
in case nobody knows which way to run.

640
00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:27.520
The fire inspector then arrived.

641
00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:29.238
- Jeremy. Nice to meet you.
- Fire officer.

642
00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:31.560
And though Alan
had been very thorough.

643
00:32:31.760 --> 00:32:35.000
this chap reckoned
we could go further still.

644
00:32:35.200 --> 00:32:39.040
I would maybe just suggest
some emergency lighting on the outside

645
00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:41.280
to cover any darkness.

646
00:32:41.480 --> 00:32:43.320
I'm sorry. light out here?

647
00:32:43.480 --> 00:32:44.520
Yeah. emergency lighting.

648
00:32:44.680 --> 00:32:48.800
So if it's dark outside
and people obviously have to evacuate.

649
00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:51.920
You know it's not a Boeing 747 this?
It's just a shop.

650
00:32:52.120 --> 00:32:53.320
Yeah.

651
00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:58.040
Bottom line. though.
the fire inspector was happy

652
00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:01.960
and the roof was how the council
wanted it to be.

653
00:33:02.120 --> 00:33:04.520
All we needed now. then.
was planning permission

654
00:33:04.680 --> 00:33:08.280
to put down some gravel
to make a car park.

655
00:33:09.520 --> 00:33:12.480
Uh-oh.
Charlie's got a notepad and pen.

656
00:33:12.680 --> 00:33:13.960
And a couple of days later.

657
00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:17.280
Charlie dropped round
with the planner's decision.

658
00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:22.320
"Would not be compatible
or consistent in scale

659
00:33:22.480 --> 00:33:24.720
"with the existing farming business..."

660
00:33:24.880 --> 00:33:26.438
- They didn't give us an alternative.
- "Visibly intrusive..."

661
00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:28.760
They simply say at the top that the...

662
00:33:28.960 --> 00:33:32.200
- "Notice."
- Oh. "Hereby refuses the application."

663
00:33:32.360 --> 00:33:34.400
They can't refuse the car park.
Charlie.

664
00:33:34.600 --> 00:33:35.878
Well. they've refused everything now.

665
00:33:35.920 --> 00:33:38.560
They've refused the restaurant.
the car park.

666
00:33:38.760 --> 00:33:40.160
They've just refused everything.

667
00:33:40.360 --> 00:33:43.800
The strange thing about this decision
is that this is...

668
00:33:43.960 --> 00:33:45.358
They've just done this
without the committee.

669
00:33:45.400 --> 00:33:46.880
They've done this
without committee.

670
00:33:47.040 --> 00:33:49.760
So they haven't asked councillors.
they've just decided on their own.

671
00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:52.320
Do you know all planning applications
are meant to be treated

672
00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:54.760
in a positive and creative manner?

673
00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:56.680
You know. for farmers.
the government policy

674
00:33:56.800 --> 00:34:01.720
is supporting social and economic
development. wherever it is.

675
00:34:01.880 --> 00:34:03.680
as long as it's sensitive
to the environment.

676
00:34:03.840 --> 00:34:05.240
But we've still ended up with that!

677
00:34:05.320 --> 00:34:06.400
- Yes.
- I know.

678
00:34:06.560 --> 00:34:08.240
And we're always going
to end up with this.

679
00:34:08.320 --> 00:34:11.040
A council have decided there will be

680
00:34:11.200 --> 00:34:15.680
no more development of any sort
at Diddly Squat.

681
00:34:19.160 --> 00:34:21.920
To get a car park. then.
and. more importantly.

682
00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:24.400
a restaurant where we could sell
our meat.

683
00:34:24.560 --> 00:34:27.640
we'd have to appeal to the government.

684
00:34:28.200 --> 00:34:30.160
And you're not really seeing it
at its best

685
00:34:30.320 --> 00:34:33.400
'cause you're looking into the sun.
But it's a hell of a view. that.

686
00:34:33.560 --> 00:34:35.160
And to help us do that.

687
00:34:35.320 --> 00:34:38.360
Charlie had finally managed
to assemble a team

688
00:34:38.520 --> 00:34:40.680
of legal Avengers.

689
00:34:40.920 --> 00:34:42.920
If you have evidence on footfall.

690
00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:45.080
on usage. on turnover
and all that sort of thing

691
00:34:45.200 --> 00:34:48.200
from last year's operation.
that's going to be very. very important.

692
00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:50.840
Where's the nearest footpath
in that direction?

693
00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:52.880
- There isn't anything.
- There is no footpath.

694
00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:55.840
So the road. the visual receptor
would be the road.

695
00:34:56.000 --> 00:34:56.958
Yeah. something like that.

696
00:34:57.000 --> 00:34:59.478
And the building. I take it. was built
in accordance with the plans?

697
00:34:59.520 --> 00:35:01.000
- There's no issue about that?
- Yeah.

698
00:35:01.880 --> 00:35:04.520
Once the legal team
had assessed the situation.

699
00:35:04.640 --> 00:35:06.800
they laid out their plan.

700
00:35:06.960 --> 00:35:08.840
which was impressive.

701
00:35:09.040 --> 00:35:10.680
So you appeal
to the Secretary of State.

702
00:35:10.880 --> 00:35:12.960
The Secretary of State appoints
a planning inspector.

703
00:35:13.040 --> 00:35:16.760
We'll be going for a public enquiry.
so he convenes a public enquiry

704
00:35:16.920 --> 00:35:18.360
in an appropriate room.

705
00:35:18.520 --> 00:35:21.520
And he hears the evidence. he hears
the advocates. he hears the submissions.

706
00:35:21.640 --> 00:35:23.480
We're going to have a landscape bod.

707
00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:26.200
We're going to have a transport bod.

708
00:35:26.440 --> 00:35:28.360
We're going to have a planning bod.

709
00:35:28.520 --> 00:35:30.760
We might have a farm business bod.

710
00:35:31.520 --> 00:35:33.600
So. what's that. four witnesses so far?

711
00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:36.040
- Lighting.
- Oh. lighting.

712
00:35:36.200 --> 00:35:39.360
There may be a technical
lighting bod involved.

713
00:35:39.520 --> 00:35:41.960
So that's four to five witnesses for us.

714
00:35:42.120 --> 00:35:44.840
We can expect the authority
to match those.

715
00:35:45.040 --> 00:35:46.800
We've then got a row of third parties.

716
00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:48.840
who. I mean. someone like Mr Dewar

717
00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:51.640
might decide he'd like to be
what's called a Rule 6 party.

718
00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:53.480
which is where he takes
a formal position.

719
00:35:53.640 --> 00:35:55.840
He's the one who had
the council's opinion. isn't he?

720
00:35:56.000 --> 00:35:56.900
Yeah.

721
00:35:56.960 --> 00:36:00.160
So you can well imagine that he might
decide to instruct planners.

722
00:36:00.360 --> 00:36:03.320
landscape bods and turn up
as another party.

723
00:36:03.480 --> 00:36:07.440
So we're then looking
two to three weeks of enquiry.

724
00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:12.080
Everything he was saying
was leading me to one question.

725
00:36:12.280 --> 00:36:15.440
Broadly speaking. how much does it cost?

726
00:36:15.640 --> 00:36:17.480
I did give a figure to Mark.

727
00:36:17.640 --> 00:36:19.120
Which was?

728
00:36:19.880 --> 00:36:21.840
It was on the basis
of a one-week enquiry.

729
00:36:22.040 --> 00:36:24.200
How much would a one-week enquiry be?

730
00:36:24.440 --> 00:36:29.080
Probably around two hundred...
to two fifty.

731
00:36:29.200 --> 00:36:31.680
So a three-week enquiry
would be?

732
00:36:31.920 --> 00:36:34.640
There'll be a larger brief for you
and then you have more refreshers.

733
00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:36.160
It's going to... So on that...

734
00:36:36.320 --> 00:36:37.360
And someone from us. So...

735
00:36:37.440 --> 00:36:39.200
It's going to be £300.000.

736
00:36:39.400 --> 00:36:41.160
If it's that long. yeah.

737
00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:43.000
You talk about 300.000.

738
00:36:43.160 --> 00:36:45.280
there are obviously
the consultants' fees.

739
00:36:45.440 --> 00:36:48.440
Because each one's gonna have
to produce a proof of evidence.

740
00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:51.760
- various documents go with it...
- So that's more?

741
00:36:51.920 --> 00:36:53.080
So more than 300.000?

742
00:36:53.200 --> 00:36:54.880
300.000. that's the legal.

743
00:36:55.040 --> 00:36:58.800
So QC. us. and then the witnesses.

744
00:36:58.960 --> 00:37:00.680
The witnesses are all going
to have to be...

745
00:37:00.920 --> 00:37:02.078
It's gonna be half a million.

746
00:37:02.120 --> 00:37:04.320
It is going to be half a million.
isn't it?

747
00:37:04.480 --> 00:37:06.280
- When all is said and done.
- Yeah.

748
00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:21.080
OK. that's that. then.

749
00:37:21.600 --> 00:37:24.440
You know. with that level of cost.

750
00:37:24.600 --> 00:37:28.960
you know. there's no chance ever
of making. you know. a profit.

751
00:37:29.120 --> 00:37:33.680
No matter. you know.
how good the business plan is.

752
00:37:33.880 --> 00:37:37.480
Half a million pounds to fund an appeal
for the Secretary of State

753
00:37:37.640 --> 00:37:39.040
